\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We have identified a systematic validity gap in VLA collision avoidance evaluation. Across the Pi0 model family, 98--99\% of episodes counted as safe under standard CAR are attributable to action collapse rather than obstacle avoidance. The displacement-filtered metric CAR$_\text{f}$ separates genuine avoidance from inaction and should be adopted as a complement to raw CAR in future evaluations.

Two structural findings reinforce this conclusion. The anti-correlation between task capability and reported safety (Section~\ref{sec:tsr_car}) means that raw CAR comparisons across models of different capability levels are misleading. The oracle SDF experiments (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}) establish that end-effector-only refinement cannot ensure full-body collision avoidance, even with perfect distance information.

% TODO: Expand with Claim 4 results when available.

\paragraph{Limitations.} Our empirical evidence for fake safety comes from the Pi0 model family evaluated on the SafeLIBERO benchmark (object\_pudding Level~I). While we argue the mechanism generalizes to any VLA susceptible to action collapse, verification on other architectures and benchmarks remains future work. The displacement threshold $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm is empirically robust (Appendix~\ref{app:threshold_sensitivity}) but task-dependent in principle. The oracle experiments test only end-effector-level SDF refinement; full-body SDF approaches may yield different conclusions.

